# The SERIES chapter for the yamlover screencasts — a directory chapter whose positional body
# points at one subdirectory per presentation (`- *: 01-…`), the shape of
# ../examples/66-pet-keeper-handbook. This file IS the unified plan: the format every deck
# follows, the deck table, and the recording checklist. Each deck is a self-contained folder —
# zip it and send it. Specs: ../CHAPTER.md (the document model), ../MARKLOWER.md (the prose).
#
# The BODY POINTERS at the foot carry the series ORDER (what the tree lists, and in which order).
# The table is the CLICKABLE index: its deck cells are marklower links written DOCUMENT-relative
# (`:00-template`, MARKLOWER.md §Link targets), so they resolve whether this repository or this
# `slides/` folder is the served root. A cell opening with `[` must be quoted — bare, it would
# lex as a flow sequence.
!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
The yamlover screencast series
description: Short, self-contained screencasts — one idea each, recorded over the app itself
- |
  Each deck is **one folder** holding **one file**: a yamlover *chapter* whose subchapters are
  the slides. The app that renders them is the app they are about, so the projector and the
  subject are the same program.
- The format every deck follows
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - one topic, **seven slides**, four to six minutes
  - a slide is a **title** plus at most forty words — the screen shows the app, not the text
  - what to *say* lives in a `notes:` field on the slide, and never renders on it
  - structure comes from the taxonomy — a table is a `table` node, a list is a `bullets` node
  - only what ships gets demoed; anything still planned is called planned
- The series
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - '#'
      width: 1
    - Deck
      width: 4
    - What the viewer walks away with
      width: 7
    - Status
      width: 2
  - - '01'
    - '[YAML vs yamlover](:01-yaml-vs-yamlover)'
    - You already know the syntax; here are the four places the meaning differs
    - written
  - - '02'
    - '[Mind-management in small doses](:02-mind-management)'
    - Capture, outline, decide, track, tag, find — each one a plain file
    - written
  - - '03'
    - '[Files and folders as the database](:03-files-as-database)'
    - Concretes, the supersession lattice, and the two overlays
    - scaffold
  - - '04'
    - '[Tags and annotations](:04-tags-and-annotations)'
    - Filing without folders, and marking up a region of anything
    - scaffold
  - - '05'
    - '[Chapters and marklower](:05-chapters-and-marklower)'
    - Why the prose format refuses headings, and what carries them instead
    - scaffold
  - - '06'
    - '[Pointers and the graph](:06-pointers-and-the-graph)'
    - When a tree is not enough — shared nodes, DAGs, and path anchors
    - scaffold
  - - '07'
    - '[The projectional editor](:07-the-projectional-editor)'
    - Editing structure instead of text, one cell at a time
    - scaffold
  - - '00'
    - '[The template](:00-template)'
    - A three-slide deck that explains the skeleton — copy the folder and rewrite
    - written
- Recording checklist
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: numbered>
  - run `npm start -- .` from the repository root and open `http://localhost:5173`
  - open the deck, e.g. `/slides/01-yaml-vs-yamlover` — the left tree is its slide list
  - click a slide to project it as its own page; that URL is the slide's shareable address
  - zoom the browser until the slide fills the frame — that is why slides stay short
  - keep `notes:` open on the second monitor, in the `yamlover` tab or in the editor
  - add `?depth=1` to read a whole deck as one scrolling page while rehearsing
